cartridge "POWERBALL/GAZETTE/1987"

' POWERBALL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Powerball", COMPUTE!'s Gazette May 1987
' (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, files POWERBALL.BOOT and
' POWERBALL; no author is named on the disk). Made for Amy Studio by
' Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/dis.txt, and watched in jsc64), the game is built for
' the ColecoVision: bevelled bricks, steel walls, smooth sprites, a sound
' for every event, a title tune.
'
' The game: a breakout with extras. Knock out every grey and green brick
' of a screen with the ball; steel never breaks. Green bricks break at
' once (4 points); a hit on a grey or coloured brick (1 point) gives it a
' new colour, green one time in two. A broken green brick drops a capsule
' three times in four (if none is falling); catch it with the paddle:
'   S slow: every ball back to the slowest speed
'   C catch: the paddle holds the ball until the button (or 1.5 s)
'   L long paddle         3 three balls (when only one is in play)
'   F fire: the button shoots upward; a shot breaks green bricks
'     (3 points) and recolours the others
'   N next screen (not on the tenth)
'   + one paddle more (at most 9; rarer each time)
'   P powerball: the ball ploughs through every brick and steel wall,
'     3 points a brick, 1 a steel block
' A new capsule ends the previous one. Three drones drift down from the
' top and roam; one that touches the paddle, a shot or a ball is destroyed
' (1 point, it turns the ball back) and another comes from the top, now
' and then with a capsule. Lose every ball and you lose a paddle; you
' start with 4. A cleared screen gives 100 x (screen speed + 1) points.
' The ball speeds up at random, the first time after 46 seconds of a life,
' then sooner and sooner until every second.
'
' From the original (orig/): the ten screens (narrowed from 40 to 28
' columns), the three brick kinds and the recolouring hit, the scores
' (4, 1, 3, 1, bonus 100 x (level + 1)), the eight capsules, their odds
' (S 3/4, N 3/16 halving each time in a game, + 1/2 halving within a
' screen, never the same twice in a row), their effects and the end of
' each with the next capsule, catch time, the three drones (spawn at the
' top, down-speed 1 to the level, sideways 0-15, a random push at each
' bounce, 1/8 chance of a capsule when one appears), the ball's speeds
' (8 + level at the serve, at least 2 down, steeper near the paddle's
' middle and flatter near its ends), the serve on the paddle (button or
' after 4 seconds), the speed-up timer (49, 25, 13, 7, 4, 2, then every
' period of 250 C64 passes), four paddles, at most nine, six-digit score
' and high score.
'
' Liberties: the field is 28 columns (the C64's 40 resampled) by 18 rows
' of bricks; speeds are per frame in 1/16 pixels: the C64 made about 4.3
' passes a frame, so a C64 speed V moves 2V sixteenths a frame (V 9 = 1.1
' pixels); the paddle moves 4 pixels a frame (the C64's 6 on a wider
' field was too quick to control on a joystick) and is 20 pixels wide, 32
' long (C64 24 and 48 of 320); the capsule falls half a pixel a frame;
' shots go up 8 pixels a frame (the C64's crossed the screen in a few
' frames); the drones keep a colour and spin slowly (the C64 changed their
' colour and frame at every pass, far faster than 3 Hz); the 3 capsule
' copies the ball the C64 way; the fastest ball is speed 25 (3.1 pixels
' a frame; the C64's 35 against its quicker paddle, kept in proportion); screens 1-10 repeat with the speed of their
' number (the C64 played its tenth screen at level 1 speed and the next
' round from level 2); collisions are tests on the ball's leading edge and
' boxes (the C64 compared screen cells); the "PRESS FIRE TO CONTINUE AT
' THIS LEVEL" choice after a game is gone (game over goes to the title);
' the right button pauses (the C64 paused while SHIFT was held); a SCREEN
' n banner; the capsule legend on a HOW TO PLAY page.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick moves the paddle left and
' right; a button serves a held ball and fires shots; the right button
' pauses. A button starts on the title.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

